<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the generated iOS Info.plist by Tauri, which looks for this
  filename alongside tauri.conf.json. It lives here rather than in
  gen/apple/*/Info.plist because gen/ is generated and gitignored, so an edit
  made there is lost the next time anyone runs `tauri ios init`.

  Receiving needs consent too. The local network prompt is not only about
  making outbound connections — binding a socket and taking delivery of
  Art-Net on 6454 or sACN on 5568 is just as much local network access, and
  since iOS 14 it needs the user's permission. Without this key the system has
  no string to put in the prompt, so it never asks, and the sockets bind
  successfully and then simply never receive a packet. That failure looks
  exactly like a console pointed at the wrong address, which is the last place
  anyone would think to stop looking.

  Multicast is a separate question this key does not answer. Joining a group —
  which sACN's per-universe subscriptions and CITP discovery both do — needs
  com.apple.developer.networking.multicast, an entitlement Apple grants by
  application. Until that exists, unicast Art-Net and unicast sACN work and the
  multicast paths do not; net.rs deliberately treats a failed join as
  non-fatal, so the app will look like it is working and receive nothing.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>simpleVIS receives Art-Net and sACN lighting data from a console on your local network, so it can show your rig live. Nothing is sent out and nothing leaves the device.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
